  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure will center overhead tonight, leading to \nefficient radiational cooling. Temperatures will drop into the 40s \nand 30s away from the shores of Lake Champlain. Patchy frost is \nexpected in the colder hollows of the Adirondacks and Northeast \nKingdom. As the high pushes off to the east tomorrow, warm air \nadvection will moderate the airmass. Therefore, highs will mostly be \nin the 70s for the next couple days, with lows in the 40s and 50s \nand no threats of frost. It's a great stretch of September weather \nin store. An area of rain will attempt to move into the region on \nThursday into Thursday night as isentropic lift occurs on a warm \nfront. EPS members generally favor a wetter and farther north front \nwhile GEFS and CMCE members generally keep the rain to the south. \nHowever, any precipitation would be light and stratiform, with the \nwet EPS probabilities of a half inch of rain generally ranging \nbetween 10-20 percent.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: After Thursday's precipitation, ~1025mb surface high \npressure will shift east across Quebec Province. Any showers will \ncome to an end overnight, and allow for a gorgeous a Friday. High \ntemperatures will top out in the upper 60s to mid 70s with a steady \nnorth breeze. Some high clouds will begin to stream overhead, and \nthat should keep overnight lows mostly in the 40s to lower 50s in \nthe broad valleys Friday night.\n\nFor Saturday, a warm front will be approaching. So plenty of high \nlevel clouds will be overhead. This will keep temperatures tamped \ndown. Given the extended timeframe, models diverge on how quickly \nprecipitation arrives. Channeled flow aloft will be a fast, 100-120 \nknots at 250mb just to our north are fairly fast. Cluster analysis \non the LREF depicts more uncertainty on low to mid level zonal flow. \nThe members with faster 700mb flow feature an earlier arrival of \nprecipitation with the greater moisture fluxes. Tracking rain \nthrough panels of individual ensemble members, there are a handful \nthat are very late with the arrival of rain well into Sunday. Will \nalso note that there are some members with a solid swath of rain. \nDigging through NBM probabilities, there's about a 30-40% chance of \n1+ inch of rain and a 10-25% chance of 2+ inches of rain focused \nnear or over the region Saturday into Sunday. The temperature \nforecast will be highly dependent on whether the warm front lifts \nnorth of the region or not. If it doesn't, then we'll likely \ncontinue to see 60s to near 70, and if it does, then we could see \ntemperatures on the warmer side.",
